faq:
  - q: "What should you say when you receive a job offer?"
    a: "Do not accept or counter immediately. The script: 'Thank you so much — I'm genuinely excited about this opportunity. I'd like to take 24–48 hours to review the full package before responding. Can I get back to you by [specific day]?' This buys time for research and removes the pressure of an on-the-spot decision. Never accept on the first call. Never counter on the first call without at least 24 hours of preparation."
  - q: "What should you say when making a counter offer?"
    a: "State your number, anchor to market and role scope — not personal need. Script: 'I've done my research on the market for this role and level, and based on what I've found — and the scope of what you've described — I was hoping we could get to [X]. Is that something we can work toward?' Do not explain why you need more money. Do not apologize. State the number and let them respond."
  - q: "What do you say when they say the offer is final?"
    a: "'I appreciate that — I understand there may be constraints. Given that, would there be flexibility on [signing bonus / equity / start date / title]?' Pivot to non-base levers. When the employer says 'this is our final offer,' it almost never is for every component of the package. The base may be fixed while signing bonus, equity refresh, or title is still negotiable."
  - q: "How do you negotiate salary without losing the offer?"
    a: "Negotiate professionally and specifically. A reasonable counter — 10–20% above the offer, anchored to market data — almost never loses an offer. Employers expect negotiation. The risk is not in asking — it is in how you ask. Unprofessional counters (ultimatums, repeated requests, emotional appeals) risk relationships. Specific, market-anchored counters do not."
  - q: "Should you negotiate salary by phone or email?"
    a: "Phone or video is preferred — it is harder to say no in real time and allows you to read the conversation. Email is acceptable and provides a written record. Never negotiate by text message. If the employer insists on email, use the phone script as your template and write it in a clear, professional tone."
